{"study_accession":"PRJNA630915","secondary_study_accession":"SRP260404","title":"Immunogenomic identification and characterization of granulocytic myeloid derived suppressor cells in multiple myeloma (RNA-Seq Neutrophils)","description":"Purpose: we aimed at providing the phenotypic profile of G-MDSCs based on their prognostic significance in MM, immunosuppressive potential, and molecular program. Methods and results: The pre-established phenotype of G-MDSCs was evaluated in bone marrow samples from controls and MM patients using multidimensional flow cytometry and, surprisingly, we found that CD11b+CD14-CD15+CD33+HLADR- cells overlapped with common eosinophils and neutrophils, which were not expanded in MM patients. Thus, we relied on automated clustering to unbiasedly identify all granulocytic subsets in the tumor microenvironment: basophils, eosinophils, immature, intermediate and mature neutrophils. In a series of 267 newly-diagnosed MM patients (GEM2012MENOS65 trial), only the frequency of mature neutrophils at diagnosis was significantly associated with patients’ outcome, and a high mature-neutrophils/T-cell ratio resulted in inferior progression-free survival (P<.001). Upon FACSorting of each neutrophil subset, T cell proliferation decreased in presence of mature neutrophils (0.5-fold; P=.016) and the cytotoxic potential of T cells engaged by a BCMAxCD3 bispecific antibody increased notably with the depletion of mature neutrophils (4-fold; P=.0007). Most interestingly, RNAseq of the three subsets revealed that G-MDSCs-related genes were specifically upregulated in mature neutrophils from MM patients vs controls due to differential chromatin accessibility. Conclusion: Taken together, we established a correlation between the clinical significance, immunosuppressive potential and transcriptional network of well-defined neutrophil subsets, providing for the first time, a set of optimal markers (CD11b/CD13/CD16) for accurate monitoring of G-MDCSs in MM. Overall design: Various neutrophil subsets were isolated from total BM samples of HA (N = 8) and MM patients (N = 8) and bulk RNAseq was obtained using a protocol adapted from single-cell massively parallel single-cell RNA-sequencing","pubmed":"32325491","geo_series":"","n_samples":46,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN14847705","study_accession":"PRJNA630915","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"IMM_GRAN_MM_15733","description":"IMM_GRAN_MM_15733","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM4520740","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2020-05-09","attributes":[["source_name","Bone marrow (CD11b+CD16- cells)"],["tissue","bone marrow"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR11727482","experiment_accession":"SRX8286776","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"NextSeq 500","read_count":9754611,"base_count":663313548,"first_public":"2020-05-09","experiment_title":"NextSeq 500 sequencing: GSM4520740: IMM_GRAN_MM_15733 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":true},{"sample_accession":"SAMN14847706","study_accession":"PRJNA630915","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"IMM_GRAN_H_S3","description":"IMM_GRAN_H_S3","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM4520739","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2020-05-09","attributes":[["source_name","Bone marrow (CD11b+CD16- cells)"],["tissue","bone marrow"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR11727481","experiment_accession":"SRX8286775","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"NextSeq 500","read_count":15975017,"base_count":1086301156,"first_public":"2020-05-09","experiment_title":"NextSeq 500 sequencing: GSM4520739: IMM_GRAN_H_S3 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":true},{"sample_accession":"SAMN14847707","study_accession":"PRJNA630915","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"IMM_GRAN_H_S2","description":"IMM_GRAN_H_S2","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM4520738","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_publi